---
import Layout from "../../layouts/Layout.astro";
import { getPost } from "../../data/journal";
import { site } from "../../config";

const post = getPost("jobs-that-dry-the-eyes")!;
---

<Layout
  title={`${post.title} | EyesRhythm`}
  description={post.description}
  type="article"
  published={post.date}
  image={post.cover}
  imageAlt={post.coverAlt}
>
  <main id="main">
    <header class="cover">
      <img
        src={post.cover}
        alt={post.coverAlt}
        width="1280"
        height="720"
      />
      <div class="cover__shade"></div>
      <div class="cover__copy">
        <p class="kicker"><span class="kicker__dot"></span>{post.kicker}</p>
        <h1 class="display">{post.title}</h1>
        <p class="lede">{post.description}</p>
        <p class="meta">{post.read} · 9 September 2026</p>
      </div>
    </header>

    <article class="wrap article">
      <div class="prose">
        <p>
          The workday is going well, and then it isn’t—at least not for the
          eyes.
        </p>
        <p>
          The letters on the screen lose their edge. You rub your eyes, keep
          typing, and by late afternoon your eyelids feel heavy.
        </p>
        <p>
          For people who work at a computer, this can become a familiar part
          of the day.
        </p>
        <p>
          Programmers, designers, accountants, researchers, writers and many
          other professionals spend hours looking at screens. The work is
          different, but the challenge for the eyes is often similar:
          <strong
            >long periods of concentration, fewer natural interruptions and
            less awareness of
            <a href="/journal/screens-cut-blink-rate">how often we blink</a
            >.</strong
          >
        </p>
        <p>And that last part matters more than most people realise.</p>

        <h2>The problem is not just screen time</h2>
        <p>
          When we concentrate on a screen, our blinking can become
          <a href="/journal/screens-cut-blink-rate"
            >less frequent and less complete</a
          >. Blinking helps spread tears across the surface of the eye, so
          changes in blinking can affect how well the tear film is
          maintained.
        </p>
        <p>
          Research has linked prolonged digital screen use with dry-eye
          symptoms, including dryness, burning, irritation and fluctuating
          vision. However, dry eye is a complex condition with several
          possible causes. Screen use is one contributor, not the whole
          explanation.
        </p>
        <p>
          This is why two people can spend the same eight hours at a computer
          and experience very different levels of discomfort.
        </p>
        <p>
          One may take regular breaks and blink normally. Another may become
          so absorbed in the work that the eyes receive very little
          interruption.
        </p>
        <p>
          <strong
            >The number of hours matters. But what happens during those hours
            matters too.</strong
          >
        </p>

        <h2>Which jobs are most likely to affect your eyes?</h2>
        <p>
          There is no profession that automatically causes dry eye. The more
          useful way to think about the problem is to look at jobs that
          require prolonged, uninterrupted screen use.
        </p>

        <h3>Software developers and programmers</h3>
        <p>
          A programmer may spend hours reading code, debugging errors and
          switching between windows.
        </p>
        <p>
          The work requires concentration, and it is easy to remain focused
          on the screen without noticing how long it has been since the last
          proper blink.
        </p>
        <figure class="shot">
          <img
            src="/images/jobs-code.jpg"
            alt="A man typing on an external keyboard in front of two monitors of code, looking at the screens."
            width="1280"
            height="720"
          />
          <figcaption>
            Code is close work. The lids wait while the stack trace is still
            wrong.
          </figcaption>
        </figure>

        <h3>Graphic designers, UI/UX designers and video editors</h3>
        <p>
          Designers often work with detailed images, small text, colour
          differences and precise visual adjustments.
        </p>
        <p>A few minutes can easily become an hour.</p>
        <p>
          The work may feel creative and engaging. The eyes may experience it
          as prolonged concentration.
        </p>
        <figure class="shot">
          <img
            src="/images/jobs-design.jpg"
            alt="A woman at an Apple monitor and iPad, drawing the same botanical graphic on both screens."
            width="1280"
            height="720"
          />
          <figcaption>
            Design work is a field of tiny edges. The next adjustment is
            always one click away.
          </figcaption>
        </figure>
        <figure class="shot">
          <img
            src="/images/jobs-cad.jpg"
            alt="A man at dual monitors using 3D CAD software, a mechanical part on one screen and a technical drawing on the other, webcam on the monitor."
            width="1280"
            height="720"
          />
          <figcaption>
            3D design software is the same long look: a part to rotate, a
            constraint to check, a millimetre to hunt.
          </figcaption>
        </figure>

        <h3>Data analysts, accountants and financial professionals</h3>
        <p>
          Spreadsheets, dashboards, financial models and reports can require
          long periods of uninterrupted screen work.
        </p>
        <p>
          The difficulty is often not one particularly long session, but the
          accumulation of many hours of visual attention throughout the day.
        </p>

        <h3>Writers, editors and researchers</h3>
        <p>
          Writing and research can look different from programming, but the
          eyes face a similar challenge.
        </p>
        <p>
          A writer may spend the morning reading documents, the afternoon
          drafting content and the evening reviewing it. A researcher may
          move between papers, spreadsheets and presentations without
          spending much time looking away.
        </p>
        <p>
          The result is often
          <strong
            >long periods of near work with very little visual
            interruption</strong
          >.
        </p>

        <h3>Customer-service and support professionals</h3>
        <p>
          Many customer-service jobs now involve computers for most of the
          working day.
        </p>
        <p>
          A support agent may read a customer's message, check an account,
          update a record and respond—all while remaining at the screen.
        </p>
        <p>
          The work may not feel visually demanding in the same way as design
          or programming, but the eyes are still spending much of the day
          looking at a nearby display.
        </p>

        <h3
          >Teachers, lecturers and other professionals who work with digital
          documents</h3
        >
        <p>
          Teaching and academic work increasingly involve presentations,
          online platforms, digital grading and electronic documents.
        </p>
        <p>
          A teacher may spend part of the day in a classroom and another part
          reviewing assignments or preparing material on a computer.
        </p>
        <p>
          This combination can create long periods of screen exposure that
          are easy to overlook because the work is divided into different
          tasks.
        </p>

        <h2>Why does screen work make the eyes feel dry?</h2>
        <p>
          The main issue is often <strong>tear-film instability</strong>.
        </p>
        <p>
          The surface of the eye is covered by a thin layer of tears. This
          layer helps keep the eye comfortable and the vision clear.
        </p>
        <p>When we blink, we refresh and spread that tear film.</p>
        <p>
          But during prolonged screen work, blinking may become less frequent
          or incomplete. This can increase evaporation and leave parts of the
          eye exposed for longer.
        </p>
        <p>
          A
          <a href="https://doi.org/10.2147/OPTH.S321591"
            >review of digital screen use</a
          >
          describes blink abnormalities and tear-film changes as important
          contributors to screen-associated dry eye.
        </p>
        <p>This is also why the symptoms can come and go.</p>
        <p>
          You may feel fine in the morning, begin to notice irritation after
          several hours, and then feel better after stepping away from the
          screen.
        </p>
        <p>
          The eyes are not necessarily becoming permanently damaged. They may
          simply be struggling to maintain comfort during the task.
        </p>

        <figure class="shot">
          <img
            src="/images/jobs-tear.jpg"
            alt="Close-up of an open eye, a thin glossy tear film catching the light."
            width="1280"
            height="720"
          />
          <figcaption>
            After a blink, a thin even film sits on the eye. That is the
            surface you are looking through.
          </figcaption>
        </figure>

        <h2>The overlooked part of the problem: blinking</h2>
        <p>Most people think about screen time in terms of hours.</p>
        <p>But hours alone do not tell the whole story.</p>
        <p>
          Two people may spend eight hours at a computer and experience very
          different levels of discomfort. One may take regular breaks and
          blink normally. The other may spend long periods concentrating
          without much visual interruption.
        </p>
        <p>
          Research suggests that
          <strong
            >blink behaviour is an important part of the relationship between
            digital screen use and dry-eye symptoms</strong
          >.
        </p>
        <p>
          This is where a different way of thinking about eye comfort becomes
          useful.
        </p>
        <p>Instead of asking only:</p>
        <blockquote>
          <p>“How long have I been looking at a screen?”</p>
        </blockquote>
        <p>We can also ask:</p>
        <blockquote>
          <p>
            <strong
              >“What are my eyes doing while I am looking at it?”</strong
            >
          </p>
        </blockquote>

        <h2>Eye care begins with awareness</h2>
        <p>Most of us do not consciously control every blink.</p>
        <p>
          We blink naturally throughout the day, often without thinking about
          it. But when we become absorbed in a task, that natural rhythm can
          change.
        </p>
        <p>
          The problem is that we may not notice the change until our eyes
          begin to feel uncomfortable.
        </p>
        <p>
          A reminder to take a break can help, but a reminder based on your
          actual blinking behaviour offers a different kind of awareness.
        </p>
        <p>
          It shifts the focus from simply counting screen hours to
          understanding how your eyes are responding to the work.
        </p>

        <figure class="shot">
          <img
            src="/images/jobs-companion.jpg"
            alt="Over the shoulder of a man at a laptop, green camera light on, EyesRhythm Trace graph open on the screen."
            width="1280"
            height="720"
          />
          <figcaption>
            EyesRhythm sits in a compact window beside the work. The camera
            in the lid is how blinks are counted, on this computer.
          </figcaption>
        </figure>

        <h2>How EyesRhythm helps</h2>
        <p>
          <strong
            ><a href="/blink-rate-detector"
              >{site.name} is a blink rate detector for long screen days.</a
            ></strong
          >
        </p>
        <p>
          It is designed for people who spend much of their working day at a
          computer and want to become more aware of their blinking habits.
        </p>
        <p>
          The app uses your computer’s
          <a href="/journal/camera-setup">webcam</a> to track your blink
          rate while you work. It does not require you to stop what you
          are doing or move away from your desk. Instead, it stays beside
          your work in a compact window, helping you notice your blinking
          without becoming another distraction.
        </p>

        <h3>It looks beyond the blink count</h3>
        <p>
          <a href="/journal/count-is-not-rhythm"
            >A blink count alone does not tell the whole story.</a
          >
        </p>
        <p>
          Six blinks can arrive in a short burst, followed by a long period
          without one. Another person may blink fewer times but more evenly.
        </p>
        <p>
          EyesRhythm brings <strong>rate and regularity</strong> together in
          its <strong>Ocular Rhythm Index (ORI)</strong>, a score from 0 to
          100 that helps you understand your blink rhythm during screen work.
        </p>
        <p>
          The idea is simple:
          <strong
            >not just how often you blink, but how steadily you blink.</strong
          >
        </p>

        <h3>It watches quietly while you work</h3>
        <p>
          EyesRhythm shows your live blink cadence in a compact window.
        </p>
        <p>
          You can see your ORI, screen time and other tracking information
          while continuing with your work. The app can also provide a gentle
          visual alert or an optional sound when your blink rhythm falls
          below your configured threshold.
        </p>
        <p>
          The goal is not to interrupt your concentration. It is to help you
          become aware of what your eyes may be doing while you concentrate.
        </p>

        <h3>It helps you practise</h3>
        <p>
          EyesRhythm also includes a
          <strong>two-minute guided practice session</strong>.
        </p>
        <p>
          The idea is to make the habit easier to feel, rather than simply
          telling you to blink more. With regular practice, the aim is to
          build awareness of a healthier blinking rhythm during screen work.
        </p>

        <h3>It respects your privacy</h3>
        <p>
          EyesRhythm is designed to work locally on your computer.
        </p>
        <p>
          After license registration, the app works offline. Camera frames
          stay on your machine, and the app does not upload or keep a film of
          your face. Your daily totals and preferences remain in a local
          folder.
        </p>
        <p>
          For an app that works with a camera, that is an important part of
          the design.
        </p>

        <h2>A better way to think about screen breaks</h2>
        <p>
          The goal is not to avoid screens. For many people, that is simply
          not realistic.
        </p>
        <p>
          The goal is to make screen work less demanding on the eyes.
        </p>
        <p>
          Take short breaks. Look away from the screen. Pay attention to
          blinking. Adjust your workstation if needed.
        </p>
        <p>
          And if you spend most of your day at a computer, consider making
          eye awareness part of your work routine.
        </p>
        <p>
          <strong
            >EyesRhythm is built around that idea: a small, quiet companion
            that helps you notice your blinking while you work.</strong
          >
        </p>

        <figure class="shot">
          <img
            src="/images/jobs-away.jpg"
            alt="The same man from the banner, smiling comfortably while working at his laptop."
            width="1280"
            height="720"
          />
          <figcaption>
            Comfortable blinking is not a locked 100. It is enough blinks,
            without the long dry wait.
          </figcaption>
        </figure>

        <h2>Your eyes deserve attention too</h2>
        <p>
          The modern workplace asks the eyes to do something they were not
          designed to do continuously: remain focused on a near task for
          hours, often with fewer complete blinks.
        </p>
        <p>The answer is not to stop working.</p>
        <p>
          It is to understand how work affects the eyes—and to build better
          habits around the way we use screens.
        </p>
        <p>
          <strong
            >Your eyes are part of your working system. They deserve the same
            attention you give to your posture, your chair and your
            productivity.</strong
          >
        </p>

        <h3>A note about eye health</h3>
        <p>
          EyesRhythm is a wellness companion, not a medical device. It does
          not diagnose, treat or prevent dry eye disease.
        </p>
        <p>
          If you experience persistent dryness, pain, blurred vision or other
          eye symptoms, consult a qualified eye-care professional.
        </p>

        <h2>References</h2>
        <ol class="sources" id="sources">
          <li>
            <a href="https://doi.org/10.2147/OPTH.S321591"
              ><cite>Digital Screen Use and Dry Eye: A Review.</cite></a
            >
            Review of the relationship between digital screen use, blinking
            and dry-eye symptoms.
          </li>
          <li>
            <a href="/blink-rate-detector"><cite>EyesRhythm.</cite></a> Blink
            rate detector for Mac and Windows, features and privacy.
          </li>
        </ol>
        <p class="note">
          Related reading:
          <a href="/blink-rate-detector">Blink rate detector</a>
          ·
          <a href="/journal/screens-cut-blink-rate"
            >Why screens cut your blink rate</a
          >
          ·
          <a href="/journal/count-is-not-rhythm"
            >Why the blink count is not enough</a
          >
          ·
          <a href="/journal/camera-setup">Set up your camera so blinks count</a>
        </p>
      </div>

      <p class="back">
        <a href="/journal">All journal</a>
        ·
        <a href="/blink-rate-detector">Blink rate detector</a>
        ·
        <button type="button" data-open-purchase>{site.cta}</button>
      </p>
    </article>
  </main>
</Layout>

<style>
  .cover {
    position: relative;
    width: 100%;
    min-height: min(72vh, 640px);
    display: grid;
    align-items: end;
    color: #fff;
    background: #1d1d1f;
  }

  .cover img {
    position: absolute;
    inset: 0;
    width: 100%;
    height: 100%;
    object-fit: cover;
    object-position: 50% 28%;
  }

  .cover__shade {
    position: absolute;
    inset: 0;
    background: linear-gradient(
      180deg,
      rgba(0, 0, 0, 0.15) 20%,
      rgba(0, 0, 0, 0.62) 100%
    );
  }

  .cover__copy {
    position: relative;
    z-index: 1;
    width: min(var(--wrap), calc(100% - var(--gutter) * 2));
    margin: 0 auto;
    padding: 48px 0 40px;
    display: grid;
    gap: 12px;
    justify-items: start;
  }

  .cover .kicker {
    color: rgba(255, 255, 255, 0.86);
  }

  .cover .display {
    font-size: clamp(2.4rem, 6vw, 4.2rem);
    max-width: 16ch;
    color: #fff;
  }

  .cover .lede {
    color: rgba(255, 255, 255, 0.88);
    max-width: 34rem;
  }

  .meta {
    font-size: 0.88rem;
    color: rgba(255, 255, 255, 0.7);
  }

  .article {
    padding: 48px 0 96px;
    display: grid;
    gap: 16px;
    justify-items: start;
  }

  .shot {
    margin: 16px 0 4px;
    width: min(100%, 42rem);
  }

  .shot img {
    width: 100%;
    border-radius: 20px;
    aspect-ratio: 16 / 9;
    object-fit: cover;
  }

  .shot figcaption {
    margin-top: 8px;
    font-size: 0.88rem;
    color: var(--text-soft);
  }

  blockquote {
    margin: 20px 0;
    padding: 4px 0 4px 18px;
    border-left: 3px solid var(--blue);
    max-width: 42rem;
  }

  blockquote p {
    font-size: 1.05rem;
    line-height: 1.55;
    color: var(--text);
  }

  .prose sup a {
    color: var(--blue-deep);
    text-decoration: none;
    font-weight: 600;
  }

  .sources {
    margin: 8px 0 0;
    padding-left: 1.2rem;
    display: grid;
    gap: 12px;
    max-width: 42rem;
    color: var(--text-soft);
    font-size: 0.92rem;
  }

  .sources a {
    overflow-wrap: anywhere;
  }

  .note {
    margin-top: 24px;
    max-width: 42rem;
    color: var(--text-soft);
    font-size: 0.92rem;
  }

  .back {
    margin-top: 12px;
    font-size: 0.95rem;
  }

  .back button {
    color: var(--blue-deep);
    font-weight: 600;
  }
</style>
